  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad496817cc2ac7b498b32f0fc2de6fb3, type: 3}
  m_Name: R1_08_HeartOfTheForest
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::JournalEntry
  title: "8 \xB7 Heart of the Forest"
  pages:
  - bigIdea: A Burst gives every mass the same momentum. A slope gives every mass
      the same speed.
    notes: "The Heart Cart taught us the last trick of the Shattered Grounds. Burst
      it and it always gets 15 kg\xB7m/s, heavy or light: a heavier cart just goes
      slower. But let it roll down a slope and gravity gives it the same speed whatever
      it weighs. So the loaded cart, rolled downhill, carries twice the momentum
      of the empty one."
    lookCloser: "Down 3 m: v\xB2 = 2\xB7g\xB7h = 2 \xD7 29.43 \xD7 3, so v \u2248
      <color=#a26029>13 m/s</color> (a little less after friction).\nEmpty, 2 kg:
      p \u2248 <color=#896923>25 kg\xB7m/s</color>.    Loaded, 4 kg: p \u2248 <color=#896923>50
      kg\xB7m/s</color>."
    funFact: Drop a heavy and a light ball together and they land together (if the
      air doesn't get in the way). Galileo is said to have tried it.
    marginP1: More ore!
    marginP2: Heavier is better? For once!
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Cart

        4 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Weight
        newtons: 117.7
        direction: 3
        kind: 3
        unit: N
      - label: Momentum
        newtons: 50
        direction: 0
        kind: 5
        unit: "kg\xB7m/s"
      caption: 'Twice the mass, the same speed: twice the momentum.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: 'Wind it slowly, let it go: the hammer''s momentum passes to the cart.'
    notes: 'P2 winds the hammer up through its pulley, click by click: the ratchet
      holds it each time the cord goes back. From the balcony, P1 lets it go. It
      swings down and slams into the cart, and the cart flies off toward the ramp.
      How much momentum passes on depends on the masses: a light, empty cart shoots
      away while the hammer swings on, but a cart as heavy as the hammer takes nearly
      all of it and the hammer almost stops. So drop ore in first!'
    lookCloser: "Pulley 6:1: the 4 kg hammer weighs <color=#8a5a2e>118 N</color>,
      the cord only needs 118 \xF7 6 = <color=#a26029>19.6 N</color>.\nWound 1.2
      m: v\xB2 = 2\xB7g\xB7h = 2 \xD7 29.43 \xD7 1.2, so v \u2248 <color=#a26029>8.4
      m/s</color>: the hammer carries p \u2248 <color=#896923>34 kg\xB7m/s</color>.\nA
      4 kg cart (two ore) takes \u2248 <color=#896923>32 kg\xB7m/s</color> of it;
      an empty 2 kg cart only \u2248 <color=#896923>21 kg\xB7m/s</color>.\nFriction:
      <color=#7d3029>3 kg\xB7m/s</color> lost every second. Full: 100 kg\xB7m/s."
    funFact: 'Real pile drivers work like the hammer: lift a heavy weight slowly,
      let it fall, and it hits with a huge impulse. Newton''s cradle shows the rest:
      equal masses pass the momentum on almost completely.'
    marginP1: 'Two ore in: now it''s as heavy as the hammer!'
    marginP2: Winding... click, click, click!
    diagram:
      show: 0
      objectLabel: 2 kg
      forces: []
      caption: 
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  tip: 'Load the cart before you send it: the heavier cart is the one that brings
    the momentum. One of you works the lift and the chute, the other sends the cart
    round again. Watch the column: at the top, the engine wakes.'
  sketch: {fileID: 0}
  sketchCaption: 
